# 1 What you will build

This book teaches the reconstruction of The Human Testament application from an empty directory. Work through the chapters in order. You will build the manuscript reader and search first, then the API and account system, private reading features, community discussions, articles, notifications, analytics and administration. The last chapters take the completed project through verification and deployment.

The target is the inspected working-tree snapshot dated 14 November 2026. It includes the Vercel configuration and documentation consolidation changes, which are not all represented by the last Git commit. The reference source and checksums embedded in this PDF identify the actual edition. Copying an older repository commit is not an equivalent reconstruction.

## What you need to know

You should be comfortable with JavaScript functions, modules, promises, React components, HTTP and basic MongoDB concepts. The instructions explain project-specific decisions, interfaces and implementation order. They do not teach JavaScript syntax or database administration from first principles.

## How the book supplies the entire implementation

The PDF contains an attachment named Human-Testament-Rebuild-Kit.zip. It includes every first-party source file, both package lockfiles, tests, branding assets, the shared manuscript projection, original manuscript inputs, retained editorial evidence and the frozen candidate. Installed dependencies, live environment files, database contents and Git history are excluded. The source is divided into seven construction stages.

For each chapter, create the named files and implement the behavior described. Complete listings of foundational modules appear in the worked-code sections. Longer feature modules are supplied in full in the kit under reference, at the exact paths printed in the book. You may type the implementation or copy those reference files while studying it. No missing controller, stylesheet, dataset or image needs to be invented, and the reconstruction does not depend on having this application's Git repository.

The kit is essential to an exact reconstruction: authored manuscript text and bitmap logos cannot be recreated accurately from prose. Source listings are wrapped for reading; use the attached file when copying long JSX or command lines. The SHA 256 manifest lets you distinguish the supplied implementation from your own later changes.

## The finished system

The browser application uses React 19, Vite 8, React Router 7 and Tailwind 3. The API uses CommonJS modules, Express 5 and Mongoose 9. The browser and API share one read-only manuscript provider. MongoDB stores accounts and reader contributions; it is not required for the bundled public reader. Google Workspace email uses the Gmail HTTP API. The frontend is configured for Vercel static hosting, and the backend remains a separate service.

Milestone checks in the book are local development checks. Production domains, provider credentials, backups, account email receipt and deployed browser behavior require the final release walkthrough. No production database contents or reusable credentials are supplied.

# 2 Prepare an empty workspace

## Extract the attached reference material

Save the attached ZIP from your PDF reader's attachment pane into a new working folder. Some browser PDF viewers do not expose attachments; use a PDF reader that supports embedded files. The other attachments preserve the previous historical documentation and a source manifest.

Run the following in PowerShell after installing a compatible Node runtime. The commands create a new project beside the extracted kit, rather than changing an existing application.

```powershell
New-Item -ItemType Directory -Path Human-Testament-Lab
Set-Location Human-Testament-Lab
# Place Human-Testament-Rebuild-Kit.zip in this folder first.
Expand-Archive -LiteralPath .\Human-Testament-Rebuild-Kit.zip -DestinationPath .\kit
node --version
npm.cmd --version
node .\kit\rebuild-tools\apply-stage.cjs 1 .\app
```

Use Node 24.22.1 or a later patched 24.x version that meets the included production validator. That floor is a property of this snapshot, not a permanent security recommendation. The validator also records floors for the 22 and 26 major lines. Check supported runtime releases before a public launch. The local system runtime used in earlier project reports was older; those reports do not change the requirements of this reconstruction.

## Create the project skeleton

The root contains backend, frontend, shared and docs. The docs directories retain machine-readable evidence only. The frontend has its own package.json and ESM module setting. The backend has a separate package.json and uses CommonJS. There is no root npm workspace and no root npm start script. Run commands in the indicated package directory or pass --prefix.

Stage 1 supplies the two manifests and lockfiles, Node version file, ignore rules, frontend build configuration, example environments and README pointers. The helper refuses to overwrite a file whose bytes differ. If you type a file yourself and the helper stops there, compare it with reference before deciding how to continue. It never silently replaces your work.

```powershell
npm.cmd ci --prefix .\app\backend
npm.cmd ci --prefix .\app\frontend
```

Keep the lockfiles. Running an unversioned scaffolder or npm install with latest package names can select a different dependency graph. Fresh installs need registry access; MongoDB test tooling may also download a development database binary. The tested lockfile versions are listed in the dependency reference.

## Checkpoint

Both package installations must complete successfully. Do not run the production frontend build yet: its prebuild intentionally needs manuscript inputs and backend verification tools from the next stage. A missing ../shared or backend path at this point is an incomplete stage, not a reason to remove the verification hook.

# 3 Trace the architecture before writing features

## Follow one public read

A reader opens /testament/freedom/10/14. AppRoutes resolves the address. ChapterPage parses the parameters, calls useChapter and manages verse selection. useChapter calls chapterService through useResource. The service validates the requested address and decoded response. getContentSource chooses the bundled PDF provider or the HTTP adapter. ChapterReader renders authored verses and supporting notes.

The API path is separate: Express mounts book.routes, the controller invokes readContent.service, and that service selects the shared PDF projection or the approved database content service. Database edition checks remain enforced when that source is selected. The frontend does not query MongoDB or import backend controllers.

## Follow one private write

A signed-in reader saves a verse. The UI calls the personal library service. That service sends a restricted request through authService.privateRequest, which attaches the current in-memory bearer credential and mutation headers. Express validates the origin, session and feature prerequisites. The backend service derives ownership from authenticated server context and writes an owner-scoped record. A safe DTO is returned and decoded before the browser updates its view.

Changing a button or hiding an admin link never grants authority. The backend must independently check account state, email verification, resource ownership and any feature-specific grant. Keep these checks in the service as well as the route boundary when helpers may be invoked directly.

## Establish the folders

In backend/src, use config for runtime inputs, models for schemas, services for domain operations, controllers for HTTP adaptation, routes for endpoint assembly, middleware for request gates and utils for shared validation. The repositories directory supports canonical database content reads. Startup is in server.js; app.js assembles middleware and routes without immediately listening.

In frontend/src, pages compose screens; routes assemble navigation and access wrappers; features own reader, manuscript and home behavior; services own transport and response contracts; hooks own asynchronous resources and session subscriptions; components provide reusable layout and UI. Data files hold authored editorial copy and stable references. Styles and Tailwind configuration own the existing visual system.

## Dependency rule

Build helpers and models before services, services before route composition, and data services before page assembly. Some reference modules import features introduced later in the book. Do not replace these imports with mock success responses: complete the specified module family before exercising its checkpoint. Stage 3 installs the complete backend family; stage 5 completes frontend imports.

# 4 Rebuild the manuscript foundation

## Install the second construction stage

```powershell
node .\kit\rebuild-tools\apply-stage.cjs 2 .\app
Set-Location .\app\backend
npm.cmd run content:check
Set-Location ..\..
```

Stage 2 supplies backend/data, shared, retained JSON evidence, immutable candidate artifacts and the content verification scripts. Preserve the original bytes and filenames. The source PDF, clean DOCX and TXT, structured drafts, ingestion report and source validation report are inputs to the verification chain. A generated shared/manuscript.json alone is sufficient for a browser read, but it is not sufficient for the production prebuild verification.

## Implement the source projection

Read shared/manuscript.mjs before adding a UI. createManuscriptProvider indexes books, chapters and verses, groups notes by fixed anchors and exposes read methods. It validates positive chapter and verse numbers. Results are cloned so a consumer cannot mutate the shared corpus. Public search is built lazily through shared/search.mjs.

The dataset contains ten books, 230 chapters and 3,905 numbered references. Of these, 3,874 have authored verse text and 31 have missing_source_text status. There are 1,470 notes. A missing text slot remains addressable without an invented text property. Repeated passages retain every original address. Do not renumber, deduplicate or fill blank source references to make a screen look complete.

Implement sync-pdf-content.js as a verifier and generator. It verifies the source chain, extracts PDF page locations and constructs the bundled dataset. Its --check path compares full serialized output without writing. Regeneration is an explicit content:sync operation after an intentionally adopted source change, not an automatic repair during an ordinary build.

## Keep editorial authority separate

AUTHOR_DECISIONS.json, EDITORIAL_REVIEW.json and SOURCE_WARNING_REVIEW.json record evidence and author decisions. Acceptance of preservation does not imply database publication approval. The frozen 0.1.0-rc.1 candidate retains importApproved false and its original manifest seal. The import scaffold is not a ready importer for the full manuscript.

Editorial preparation now checks or writes JSON. When a future candidate needs readable review material, freeze-candidate-edition.js renders it from that JSON in memory. The removed Markdown files are not a runtime prerequisite. Do not recreate the old documentation folders to make checks pass.

## Checkpoint

content:check must report manuscriptMatchesPdf true and unchanged source data. Run candidate:check against the supplied independently recorded manifest hash after completing stage 3. A mismatch is a stop condition: compare supplied inputs and checksums before changing content.

# 5 Assemble the API shell and public reader

## Install the complete backend family

```powershell
node .\kit\rebuild-tools\apply-stage.cjs 3 .\app
```

Create backend/src/config/runtime.js and db.js, then the shared validation utilities and schemas. Next create services, controllers, middleware and route modules. Add app.js after those imports exist. Finally add server.js. The exact file inventory for this stage is in the construction reference.

## Start with a database-free configuration

Create backend/.env with the following development-only content. Do not copy NODE_ENV=production from .env.example into a local development file: production startup deliberately refuses promotion through dotenv.

```dotenv
NODE_ENV=development
HOST=localhost
PORT=5000
CONTENT_SOURCE=pdf
AUTH_ENABLED=false
MAIL_DELIVERY_ENABLED=false
COMMUNITY_POSTING_ENABLED=false
ADMINISTRATION_ENABLED=false
ANALYTICS_ENABLED=false
AUTH_ALLOW_INSECURE_LOCALHOST=false
AUTH_TRUST_PROXY_HOPS=0
```

Start the backend in its own terminal, from app/backend.

```powershell
npm.cmd run dev
```

In a second terminal, inspect the two probes and one reader request.

```powershell
Invoke-RestMethod http://localhost:5000/api/health
Invoke-RestMethod http://localhost:5000/api/ready
Invoke-RestMethod http://localhost:5000/api/books
Invoke-RestMethod http://localhost:5000/api/books/freedom/chapters/10/verses/14
```

Health reports process liveness. Readiness checks the enabled prerequisites. With PDF content and optional features disabled, a MongoDB URI is unnecessary. An account endpoint being unavailable in this mode is expected. Do not manufacture an authenticated user when AUTH_ENABLED is false.

## Implement HTTP adaptation

app.js applies Helmet and the configured CORS policy before bounded JSON parsing. Analytics bodies are limited to 2 KB; auth and admin bodies to 8 KB; other JSON to 1 MB. Compressed request bodies are rejected. Domain errors flow to the shared error handler. Unknown API routes return JSON 404s.

book.routes.js exposes books, chapters, verses and supporting material. The controller turns parameters into service calls and wraps successful results. readContent.service chooses the source once from CONTENT_SOURCE. It exposes asynchronous methods whether the selected implementation is the shared provider or the database repository.

## Why startup and app assembly are separate

Tests import app.js and listen on an isolated ephemeral port without invoking production startup. server.js validates configuration, sets proxy trust, connects required storage, checks readiness, sets HTTP deadlines and installs shutdown handlers. It drains pending delivery work and disconnects Mongoose on termination. Importing app.js should not connect to a real database.

# 6 Create storage schemas and required indexes

## Use the schema catalog as a construction checklist

The schema reference later in the book is extracted from the actual Mongoose models. For every model it lists collection name, paths, basic field constraints and declared indexes. Create each model from its complete reference source. The catalog is a navigation aid; custom validators, hooks, privateOwner and shared field factories remain part of the implementation.

Start with User, RefreshSession, PasswordResetToken and EmailVerificationToken. Add Bookmark, PrivateNote, ReadingProgress and ReadingHistory. Then add community and article models, followed by notification, analytics and administration records. Canonical content models describe a separately approved database edition and must not be seeded from a draft merely to satisfy readiness.

## Enforce database constraints explicitly

Most models disable automatic collection and index creation. User email uniqueness is a database index using case-insensitive collation; a pre-insert existence query cannot replace it. Opaque account credentials are hashed before storage. TTL indexes expire credential and analytics records according to their schema. Owner and chronology indexes support the exact query shapes used by private and community services.

A schema's unique option is not a complete production initialization procedure. The operator scripts create the declared prerequisites and check their presence. Application startup checks storage; it does not silently migrate an arbitrary live database.

## Choose a development database

Account and community transactions require a replica set or supported transactional cluster. A standalone mongod is not enough for the complete application. The kit includes a local development helper using the already declared mongodb-memory-server development dependency. It starts one loopback member and keeps it alive until the terminal is closed.

```powershell
# From Human-Testament-Lab, in a separate terminal:
node .\kit\rebuild-tools\start-local-database.cjs .\app
```

Copy the printed local MONGODB_URI into backend/.env when enabling accounts. The helper uses .rebuild-local-db under the new app folder. Add that directory to local ignore rules before committing development work. This is a teaching database, not production infrastructure. Tests use their own isolated databases instead of this instance.

The helper can download its MongoDB binary on a first run. If your environment uses a reviewed installed binary or a controlled version, configure the mongodb-memory-server environment accordingly. The rehearsal used the cached 8.2.6 development binary; this is a record of the local check, not a recommendation to keep that version indefinitely.

# 7 Build authentication and session transport

## Implement registration before login

Create accountInput.js, passwords.js and tokens.js. Normalize email without provider-specific dot or plus-tag rewriting. Restrict accepted registration fields to email, password and displayName. Validate the password policy and hash with the configured bcrypt work factor. Do not accept passwordHash or privileged role fields from a client.

auth.service.register validates displayName, creates a safe unverified User and requests email verification. Delivery failure does not grant verification or destroy the valid account. Duplicate normalized email produces a sanitized conflict. The response is a safe DTO rather than the raw Mongoose document.

login validates credentials, performs password work even for an unknown mailbox, verifies active account state and creates a stored session. The response supplies a short-lived access token. The refresh credential is an opaque cookie secret with a hashed server record. In this snapshot there is no refresh endpoint or silent reload recovery: a page reload requires signing in again.

## Add middleware in the correct order

authReady checks configuration and storage availability. protectMutation checks origin, JSON mutation conventions and X-CSRF-Protection. createAuthRateLimiter bounds attempts. authenticate validates bearer credentials and reloads account/session state. requireVerifiedEmail gates features that require a confirmed mailbox. The service also validates authenticated context so a missing route wrapper does not silently grant access.

The JWT does not give a permanent right to a resource. Session revocation, account deactivation and password changes must be reflected immediately by server checks. Never trust an owner ID, role or verification flag supplied in request bodies.

## Configure local accounts

Stop the API, keep the development replica-set terminal running and extend backend/.env. Generate a fresh signing secret locally with the command below and put the result only in the ignored backend environment file.

```powershell
node -e "console.log(require('node:crypto').randomBytes(48).toString('base64url'))"
```

```dotenv
AUTH_ENABLED=true
MONGODB_URI=PASTE_THE_LOCAL_REPLICA_SET_URI
AUTH_ACCESS_TOKEN_SECRET=PASTE_A_NEW_RANDOM_SECRET
AUTH_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=http://localhost:5173
AUTH_ALLOW_INSECURE_LOCALHOST=true
AUTH_COOKIE_SAME_SITE=lax
PASSWORD_RESET_FRONTEND_URL=http://localhost:5173
EMAIL_VERIFICATION_FRONTEND_URL=http://localhost:5173
MAIL_DELIVERY_ENABLED=false
```

From app/backend, initialize account and notification prerequisites, then restart the API.

```powershell
npm.cmd run auth:init
npm.cmd run notifications:init
npm.cmd run dev
```

These initialization commands write collections/indexes to the database named by your new local URI. They seed no real account and import no manuscript. Use the supplied integration tests for complete verification/reset flows without sending real email.

# 8 Add recovery verification and account lifecycle

## Implement the common token mechanism

Account tokens use separate recovery and verification purposes. The server generates opaque secrets, stores hashes, checks expiry and consumes tokens atomically. The browser receives links with tokens in URL fragments. readAccountToken reads the credential and scrubAccountLink removes it from the browser location so it does not remain in history or get copied into application state unnecessarily.

Build accountTokenSchema.js, accountToken.service.js and accountStorage.js before the verification and password-recovery services. A password reset must update the credential and revoke affected sessions as one coherent operation. An old link cannot be reused. A verified account cannot gain a second unrelated ownership claim from resubmitting a token.

## Implement the account endpoints

forgot-password takes email and returns a generic acknowledgement. reset-password takes token, newPassword and passwordConfirmation. change-password takes currentPassword, newPassword and passwordConfirmation and authenticates the current session. verify-email takes token; resend-verification takes email and is rate limited independently. Keep these exact field names when wiring forms.

Create the email templates and email.service adapter. The Gmail adapter exchanges its refresh credential over HTTPS, caches the resulting access token briefly and sends a base64url MIME message through users/me/messages/send. It rejects header injection and does not log credential-bearing provider bodies. Provider acceptance and inbox receipt are different observations.

## Keep a deterministic development checkpoint

Do not add a console transport that prints reset links. Run the supplied frontend/backend integration test after stage 6 is installed. It mocks delivery in memory, creates a disposable transactional database, registers an account, consumes verification and recovery links, and proves reuse rejection and session revocation over real HTTP.

```powershell
# From app/frontend, after construction stage 6:
node --test tests/auth-integration.test.mjs
```

The actual provider configuration is introduced in the deployment chapters. Tests that intercept delivery do not establish that a real inbox receives mail. Keep the account lifecycle implementation unchanged when swapping test doubles for the configured provider.

# 9 Construct the frontend foundation and visual system

## Install the fourth and fifth construction stages

```powershell
# From Human-Testament-Lab:
node .\kit\rebuild-tools\apply-stage.cjs 4 .\app
node .\kit\rebuild-tools\apply-stage.cjs 5 .\app
```

Stage 4 installs UI/layout primitives, styles, assets, editorial data, browser services and shared hooks. Stage 5 installs feature components, pages, route assembly and the application entry points. Complete both before starting Vite; many components intentionally share imports across these families.

## Build the visual foundation first

Preserve tailwind.config.js and the existing styles. The palette uses ivory, parchment, charcoal, forest and bronze, with separate bronze-ink for readable text. The serif family is Cormorant Garamond with Georgia fallback; the sans family is Inter with system fallbacks. Keep the source typography scale, reading widths and spacing tokens.

PageContainer owns consistent gutters and content widths. ContentPage composes an accessible main heading and body. Shared UI primitives cover buttons, fields, modal/dialog-like surfaces and loading/error/empty states. Keep each component's actual prop contract: Button accepts leftIcon and rightIcon nodes, loading and disabled flags, variant and size. Supplying an unrelated icon prop silently loses the intended affordance.

The reference assets include the existing logo variants and favicon. Keep their filenames and imported relationships. Sitemap, robots and the web manifest belong to frontend/public. An exact reconstruction uses these supplied bitmap assets; it does not approximate them with text or draw replacement branding.

## Configure development and build separately

Create frontend/.env for development.

```dotenv
VITE_CONTENT_SOURCE=pdf
VITE_API_URL=http://localhost:5000
VITE_ANALYTICS_ENABLED=false
```

For a public reader build without a hosted backend, create frontend/.env.production with the following values. Vite's production-specific file overrides the general development file. Later, real deployment environment values take precedence.

```dotenv
VITE_CONTENT_SOURCE=pdf
VITE_API_URL=
VITE_ANALYTICS_ENABLED=false
```

All VITE_ variables are public bundle inputs. The production validator accepts only the three declared names. It rejects mock content, unsafe API URLs and unsupported variables. A backend URI must omit the trailing /api because each service path already includes that prefix.

## Checkpoint

From app/frontend run npm.cmd run dev and open http://localhost:5173. Vite uses that exact host/port and refuses to choose another port if it is busy. The public home and bundled reader should render even if the API is stopped. Account and community functions need the backend configuration from earlier chapters.

# 10 Wire providers contracts and asynchronous resources

## Implement the shared browser transport

Create ApiError.js and api/client.js before feature services. The client validates the backend URL, encodes query parameters, applies a deadline, forwards cancellation, serializes JSON mutations and maps HTTP/network failures into a stable error shape. Response body parsing is covered by the deadline. Mutations are not automatically retried because a timeout can follow a committed write.

contentSource.js selects pdf, development mock or api. Production allows pdf and api only. API mode must never fall back to bundled content when a request fails, since that would disguise an outage or source mismatch. manuscriptContent.js adapts the shared provider to browser-facing errors and enriches books with the existing editorial summaries.

## Validate every decoded domain response

Testament contracts use Zod to validate book/chapter identity, unique verse numbers, source status, note anchors and cross-reference state. chapterService rejects a response for the wrong requested address. A successful 200 response alone is not sufficient evidence that the payload belongs on the current route.

useResource owns loading, settled data, errors, retry attempts and cancellation. Its resource key hides old data immediately when route/session identity changes. Cleanup ignores late results as well as aborting the request. Keep UI selection separate from fetched content; selecting a verse must not mutate the corpus.

## Trace and implement a chapter read

Create contracts and address utilities, then the book/chapter/verse services, then their hooks, then ResourceState and finally the page. The worked-code section includes the complete getContentSource, chapterService, useResource and useChapter implementations. Use them to understand how a provider result becomes a visible state.

## Checkpoint

Open /testament/freedom/10, select verse 14 and then open its direct URL. Change book/chapter selectors. Try an unknown book, invalid chapter number and verse 999. Confirm distinct loading, error, missing-reference and empty states. Test API mode against the running backend as a separate configuration; stop it and verify that a visible error replaces the request rather than a mock success.

# 11 Build home reader materials search and questions

## Compose the public home

PublicHome composes the supplied Hero, InquiryPaths, BookCollection, FeaturedVerse, LivingInquiry, PathOfInquiry, Dialogue, Sanctuary, EssayReflections, CommunitySection, PhysicalBookSection and Footer. Home switches to ReaderDashboard when useAuth reports a signed-in user. Keep editorial prompts in their own data files; they are not canonical verse text.

Implement Navbar, ReaderNavbar and AuthenticatedNavbar with the route-aware AppContent selection. Include the skip link and one main element per page. A marketing link must lead to an implemented route or a clearly unavailable destination, rather than a dead hash that appears functional.

## Build the reader screen

Testament renders the book collection. BookPage lists available chapters. ChapterPage resolves bookSlug, chapterNumber and optional verseNumber, loads navigation data and composes ChapterReader with ReaderControls. VersePage reuses the chapter reading experience rather than inventing a second verse store. useReaderSelection owns the selected verse and supporting note context.

The reader preserves missing source text slots and uncertain note anchors. ManuscriptMaterials exposes preserved sections, glossary/index material and source context. User notes and community discussions remain separate from printed supporting notes. A canonical address is a stable slug:chapter:verse identifier; it is not a MongoDB document ID.

## Add public search

Use shared/search.mjs for the public projection and search services/contracts for the frontend. Search covers verse text, notes, glossary and cross-reference material. Matching may normalize a lookup representation; displayed text and fixed source addresses remain exact. Query state belongs in URL parameters so a result can be reopened and shared.

Build SearchForm, result/category components and the page's paging/error states. Validate q, type, book, chapter, page and limit according to the implementation. A result containing blank source text is not a reason to supply a substitute quotation. Data-backed questions and question detail pages use their own editorial records and route slugs.

## Checkpoint

Open /search?q=responsibility and follow a result to its verse. Try a no-match phrase and page/category filters. Open /testament/materials and /questions directly. The full content check later verifies every chapter, selected verses, import targets and ARIA references; a home screenshot alone would miss these routes.

# 12 Connect account pages and safe session state

## Implement the browser session service

auth.service.js owns accessToken, expiry, the current safe user snapshot, listeners and a session epoch in a closure. useAuth subscribes through useSyncExternalStore. A private request validates that it still belongs to the current session before applying its result. Logging out or starting another session invalidates delayed work.

Never put bearer tokens in React context values, localStorage, sessionStorage, URL query parameters or analytics events. The UI receives a safe user snapshot. Browser cookies remain in the HttpOnly cookie jar. Access expiry clears the local session; a page reload does not silently reconstruct a session in this implementation.

## Assemble the account flows

Implement Register, Login, ForgotPassword, ResetPassword, ChangePassword, VerifyEmail and VerificationPending with the existing field validation and page structure. Keep redirect targets local and validated. A login redirect must not accept an external URL supplied through query state.

Account settings uses getProfile/updateProfile. The editable field is displayName; email, role, verification and creation metadata are not a mass-assignment surface. Profile response schemas reject private fields and invalid envelopes. Old profile requests must not show one account's data after a different account signs in.

AuthenticatedRoute supplies navigation guards for private screens. Backend checks still provide the actual access control. Capability-specific screens query actual grants instead of inferring them from a generic user role. The User model's role enum remains user; operator grants are separate records.

## Checkpoint

Register a disposable development account, log in and open /dashboard and /account/settings. Confirm the public reader remains accessible while signed out. Attempt a protected route anonymously and verify safe return navigation. Use the isolated integration test for verification and reset completion while mail is disabled. A live inbox check belongs to the configured email walkthrough later.

# 13 Add bookmarks private notes and reading history

## Implement owner-scoped persistence

Bookmark records connect an authenticated owner to a canonical verse address. PrivateNote stores the reader's exact personal text separately from source notes. Build the shared private owner plugin and owned-resource service before the endpoint-specific services. Include owner in every read/update/delete filter, even when the resource ID is syntactically valid.

The backend derives owner identity from authenticated context. Do not allow clients to submit user, owner, role, timestamps or moderation fields. A different reader's private ID returns the same not-found style as an absent record, rather than disclosing that it exists. DTOs should not echo owner or credential internals.

## Implement progress as a concurrency problem

ReadingProgress records one checkpoint per owner/book, with a revision and lastReadAt. ReadingHistory records activity only when the implemented progress write succeeds. The frontend sends chapterNumber, optional verseNumber, expectedRevision and eventId. Optimistic concurrency rejects stale updates rather than overwriting progress from another device. Event replay must not create duplicate history.

Build ReadingProgress and ReadingHistory schemas, readingStorage prerequisites, reading.service transactions and then the routes. On the frontend add reading.service, useReadingActivity and checkpoint/history components. Use readingLocationUrl to build a reader address. Do not send private history into the public search index or analytics stream.

## Checkpoint

Save a verse, add a note, reopen it and delete only your own record. Continue reading from /dashboard and inspect /reading/history. In tests, use two accounts and a stale revision to prove owner isolation, 404 concealment, replay handling and conflict behavior. Verify that a conflict leaves the visible current checkpoint available for deliberate user choice.

# 14 Implement discussions circles and moderation

## Create contribution models independently

Discussion, Comment, ReadingCircle and CircleMembership hold reader contributions. CommunityModerator stores a separate global grant. ContentReport and ModerationAction record review and audit behavior. None of these collections changes canonical verses or source notes.

Build shared community field validators and passage-reference validation first. A discussion may point to a canonical passage, and its book/chapter/verse references must agree. Visibility public or circle must match whether a circle exists. Comments are flat records rather than an unrestricted nested tree.

## Enforce the permission lifecycle

Anonymous visitors can read eligible public contributions. Private-circle discovery and membership depend on the implemented owner/member/invitation state. Circle roles do not become global moderator permission. Editing, locking, hiding, deleting, membership changes and report review must use server checks and transaction coordination, not UI-only controls.

community.service evaluates visibility and status before returning DTOs. Queries for lists and counts must share the same visibility filters, otherwise totals can leak inaccessible records. Changes that affect membership or moderation can race with reads/writes; preserve the existing transaction and coordination patterns from the full reference module.

## Initialize and enable deliberately

Stop the API and initialize local community/article storage from app/backend. The article prerequisite is included because enabling posting checks both feature families.

```powershell
npm.cmd run community:init
npm.cmd run articles:init
```

Set COMMUNITY_POSTING_ENABLED=true in the new local backend environment only after account prerequisites are ready. Restart the API. Global moderator provisioning is a separate operator action; the included community:moderator script validates its exact arguments. Use a verified disposable local account and the script's source/usage message rather than assigning a privileged User.role.

## Build the frontend

Create the restricted community service and identity hook, then common frames/cards/forms, then Community, Discussions, DiscussionPage, ReadingCircles, ReadingCirclePage and Moderation. Render user contributions as text. Do not use dangerouslySetInnerHTML for a plain-text discussion body. Hide unavailable actions using capabilities, and still handle 403/404 responses from the server.

## Checkpoint

Read a public thread while signed out. Test verified posting, comment editing, a private circle, invitation/membership changes and report review with separate local actors. The automated community tests exercise transaction races and private visibility. A single successful create request is not enough to validate this feature.

# 15 Build articles and publication review

## Keep drafts and publications distinct

Article stores the current author-managed contribution. ArticlePublication records immutable approved snapshots. Implement articleContent validators and articleSlug helpers before the article service. Article body/title/cover/reference validation belongs on the server and is mirrored by browser contracts for feedback.

The lifecycle uses the exact stored states draft, pending_review, rejected, published and archived. Submitting a draft enters pending_review. Editing and submission use revisions so an author cannot silently replace a moderator-reviewed draft. The author cannot approve their own submission. Public lists/details must expose only approved publication data and must not leak draft text or private review reasons.

Build createDraft, mine, ownArticle and edit before submit/review. Then implement public listing/detail and publication history. Moderation reuses the existing community grant model and audit behavior. Published contributions remain separate from canonical content; publishing an article never publishes a manuscript edition.

## Build the screens in lifecycle order

Articles lists public items. ArticlePage shows one public slug. MyArticles lists the current author's records. ArticleEditorPage composes the editor, dirty-state tracking, save, submit, archive and publication history. ArticleModeration provides the peer-review queue and explicit decisions. Preserve the current pages' unsaved-edit warnings and safe state/error handling.

Remote cover images are restricted by ARTICLE_COVER_IMAGE_ORIGINS and the raster URL policy. There is no upload service in this snapshot. Do not add an undocumented file upload or render arbitrary user HTML to fill that gap.

## Checkpoint

With synthetic verified local accounts, save a draft, submit it, reject it with a reason, revise it and submit again. Have a permitted different moderator approve it. Confirm the public slug resolves the approved snapshot, not an unpublished draft, and that stale expectedRevision values fail. Check public payloads for review-reason and credential leakage.

# 16 Add notifications without coupling delivery to email

## Build domain events and private reads

Notification stores a recipient, bounded event metadata, actor/reference fields, optional deduplication identity and read timestamps. It is an in-app activity record, not an email queue. Events cover supported discussion replies, article review/publication outcomes, circle changes and report resolution.

Create notificationStorage, notificationEvents, notification.service and notificationResource before routes. Persist events in the same transaction as the supported domain mutation when required by the implementation. Do not introduce an independent background writer that can claim an event happened before the original transaction committed.

## Scope navigation and counts

Notification lists, unread counts and read mutations are owner-scoped. The server rechecks whether the current recipient can still access the target before offering a destination. Removed membership or hidden content must not remain discoverable through a notification URL. Page totals, unread counts and detail state should apply the same privacy rules.

On the frontend create the notification service, nav indicator and /notifications page. Marking an item read uses the existing empty JSON mutation body and CSRF/session transport. Session changes clear pending notification state. The browser should distinguish an empty list from an unavailable service.

## Checkpoint

Trigger a supported event between two local accounts. Confirm only the recipient sees the record. Mark it read, reload the list and repeat the read operation. Revoke access to a private-circle target and confirm the notification no longer exposes a working private destination. Verify no supported notification path changes manuscript text or sends an unexpected email.

# 17 Implement opt in analytics and permissioned reports

## Build collection independently of reading progress

AnalyticsEvent stores bounded, sanitized page/event observations with retention. AnalyticsState coordinates collection behavior; AnalyticsGrant stores operator-managed owner/admin grants. Authenticated identity is not a requirement for anonymous page collection, and anonymous identifiers must not become account identity.

Build the server analytics configuration, sanitization, storage and report service. Raw private URLs, query strings, fragments, recovery links, emails, personal notes and reader history must not become analytics payloads. The backend checks the configured frontend origins and records only the supported event schema.

The frontend tracker is disabled unless VITE_ANALYTICS_ENABLED=true. It honors the implemented DNT/GPC and consent behavior, counts SPA path transitions and clears identifiers when consent is withdrawn. Do not automatically opt users in merely because the backend has been enabled.

## Initialize and grant reports

From app/backend, analytics:init creates the declared analytics prerequisites. Enabling ANALYTICS_ENABLED also requires MongoDB, an origin allowlist and retention settings. Granting report access is an operator-managed action on a verified active account; User.role remains user.

```powershell
npm.cmd run analytics:init
# Replace USER_ID using a verified synthetic account in your local database.
npm.cmd run analytics:role -- grant owner USER_ID "local operator" "local report exercise"
```

Choose actual allowed origins and retention in the backend environment, then enable the browser build separately. Owner/Admin report screens call authenticated aggregate endpoints. They do not return a dump of individual events or private reader behavior.

## Checkpoint

Without consent, confirm no collection request occurs. With permitted local consent, navigate twice and verify the tracker records transitions according to its deduplication rules. Withdraw consent and confirm collection stops. Test reports with an ordinary account, a granted owner and a granted admin. The anonymous ingestion path must not clear the reader's authenticated session on an unrelated failure.

# 18 Add administration with explicit grants

## Separate administration from a generic role

AdministrationState serializes permission/account changes. Administration access requires an active verified User, an unrevoked admin AnalyticsGrant and administration=true. An owner grant or a moderator grant does not satisfy this check. App navigation should reflect the real capability response, while every server operation checks it again.

Build administrationStorage and administrationAccess before the administration service/router. Preserve the transaction that guards last-administrator decisions. If two administrators each try to deactivate or revoke the other, the coordination state prevents both operations from removing all active access.

## Initialize the local exercise

```powershell
# From app/backend, against your new local development database:
npm.cmd run admin:init
npm.cmd run analytics:role -- grant admin TARGET_USER_ID "local operator" "local admin exercise"
npm.cmd run admin:access -- grant TARGET_USER_ID ACTOR_USER_ID "local operator" "enable local administration"
```

Both IDs must refer to the appropriate active verified synthetic accounts, and the target must have the existing admin grant. admin:init initializes administration prerequisites, including its required grant/audit coordination records. Study the operator script and administrationAccess.provision before running it. No browser registration field can substitute for this path.

Set ADMINISTRATION_ENABLED=true only after initialization, then restart the API. Admin pages cover overview, users, account detail/status, article/community management, reports and audit. They are not a generic database editor. Canonical content has no administration write endpoint in this application.

## Checkpoint

Verify a normal reader is denied even if they manually open /admin. Verify a correctly granted admin sees the supported overview and audit records. Exercise deactivation/reactivation and revocation only with local synthetic accounts. The last active administrator protection and stale-session denial must continue to pass the regression suite.

# 19 Assemble every route and navigation state

## Complete the application entry points

main.jsx mounts App in StrictMode and imports the Tailwind entry. App owns BrowserRouter. AppContent chooses reader/public/authenticated navigation, includes the skip link, renders AppRoutes, mounts optional analytics tracking and provides the toaster. Pages should not create competing top-level BrowserRouter instances.

Build AppRoutes after every imported page exists. Its reader addresses, auth routes, personal screens, community paths, article paths and nested admin routes are listed in the route reference. Register fixed article paths such as mine/write/moderation alongside the slug route with the intended router specificity. Keep /read and /explore redirects and the legacy /books/:bookId adapter.

## Avoid promoting scaffolds into features

The source tree includes legacy/empty placeholders and compatibility adapters. A filename under pages is not proof that a route is implemented. Use the actual AppRoutes catalog to determine what the browser exposes. Do not automatically add empty commerce, glossary or path screens to the navbar simply because their files exist.

The fallback is Page unavailable with a recovery path. The app has distinct authenticated navigation and dashboard behavior. Preserve the one-main/one-heading and focus targets that the route checks exercise. Mobile navigation, skip-link focus and direct nested route loads still require browser review.

## Checkpoint

Refresh a reader verse, search, login, article-management and nested admin URL directly. Vite handles local SPA fallback; the production host needs its own rewrite. Test keyboard focus, responsive widths and error states. Route rendering tests prove module/semantic behavior, not complete browser interaction or deployed cookies.

# 20 Run the reconstruction verification gates

## Install the test stage

```powershell
# From Human-Testament-Lab:
node .\kit\rebuild-tools\apply-stage.cjs 6 .\app
node .\kit\rebuild-tools\verify-files.cjs .\app 6
```

The final argument checks all files through construction stage 6. The 485 source files present at this point must report no missing or changed entries. Stage 7 later adds frontend/vercel.json and the complete check then covers all 486 source files. Local environment and installed dependencies are intentionally outside the manifest.

## Verify backend behavior and source fidelity

Run the following from app/backend. Tests create disposable local fixtures; keep your actual service stopped if your machine cannot support several MongoDB processes at once.

```powershell
npm.cmd test -- --test-concurrency=1
npm.cmd run content:check
npm.cmd run ingestion:check
npm.cmd run editorial:check
npm.cmd run candidate:check -- --version 0.1.0-rc.1 --manifest-sha256 b8207096f76054487efa620b308b4a37ef799597c6a89b90aeb3b97f5ec9d308
```

The candidate check verifies sealed artifacts without importing them. import:check validates the legacy scaffold and is not evidence that the full candidate can be published. Do not invoke an importer with write intent to finish a local reconstruction.

## Verify browser code and route rendering

Run from app/frontend. The production-specific environment from chapter 9 avoids accidentally bundling the local development API URL.

```powershell
npm.cmd test
npm.cmd run lint
npm.cmd run build
npm.cmd run check:home
npm.cmd run check:content
```

The build first verifies the manuscript. The content script exercises all chapter routes, selected first/last/blank verse states, search, materials and import/ARIA references. A bundle-size warning is currently expected because the manuscript and several branding images are large; do not suppress it by removing content or raising the threshold as an undocumented fix.

The homepage checker counts the reading-circle cards alongside the other card collections. It also verifies that dialogue links reach implemented screens; only an unknown route should render the unavailable-page recovery state. Keep these checks aligned with the actual route map when extending the home page.

## Diagnose a failed gate

First identify whether the failure concerns missing stage files, dependency installation, source fidelity, response contracts, a database fixture, runtime validation or browser behavior. Compare the manifest before editing application logic. The troubleshooting chapter maps common symptoms to their actual owning layer.

# 21 Configure real email and a hosted backend

## Finish provider setup with new credentials

The code uses Gmail API HTTPS delivery, not SMTP credentials. Create a Google Cloud project for the intended Workspace mailbox, enable Gmail API and configure the consent/client authorization appropriate to that Workspace. Request only the sending scope required by the adapter. The provider's OAuth setup and scope references are linked in the external references appendix.

Authorize the mailbox and obtain the server-side OAuth client ID, client secret and refresh token. Set GMAIL_CLIENT_ID, GMAIL_CLIENT_SECRET, GMAIL_REFRESH_TOKEN, MAIL_FROM, MAIL_PROVIDER=gmail and MAIL_DELIVERY_ENABLED=true in the backend host's secret store. MAIL_FROM must be the authorized mailbox or configured send-as identity. Do not put any of these private credentials in Vercel VITE_ variables.

Configure PASSWORD_RESET_FRONTEND_URL and EMAIL_VERIFICATION_FRONTEND_URL to the exact HTTPS frontend origin. Those origins must also be allowed by auth configuration. A successful OAuth exchange is not enough: register a disposable staging account, inspect actual inbox receipt, consume the verification link, request/reset the password and prove reuse/session revocation behavior.

## Prepare backend runtime configuration

Deploy backend with externally supplied NODE_ENV=production. The server ignores local dotenv files in production. Select a runtime that passes supportedRuntime, configure HOST/PORT for the actual host's ingress, supply the intended MongoDB connection and exact origin/proxy/cookie settings. Community and administration require their explicit flags and initialized storage. Analytics requires its own allowlist and retention policy.

Use a real transactional database for accounts/community. Run the existing initialization scripts as an operator step against the selected environment; startup should then assert readiness without creating indexes automatically. A database with the right URI but missing prerequisites should fail readiness rather than give partial success.

## Verify the service boundary

Check /api/health and /api/ready over the hosted HTTPS endpoint. Test the actual frontend origin, preflight requests and rejected unrelated origins. Review secure cookie behavior for the chosen domain arrangement, including third-party-cookie restrictions. Choose SameSite and proxy hops for your real topology; copying localhost settings to production is invalid.

Hosting setup does not erase historical release findings. Before public operation, use new server credentials, an identified release snapshot, monitoring ownership, a tested backup/restore procedure and browser acceptance. The reconstruction kit contains no live database backup and does not restore user accounts or private reader contributions.

# 22 Deploy the frontend on Vercel

## Install the final construction stage

```powershell
# From Human-Testament-Lab:
node .\kit\rebuild-tools\apply-stage.cjs 7 .\app
node .\kit\rebuild-tools\verify-files.cjs .\app
```

The full manifest should now match. Place your downloaded textbook under app/output/pdf with its existing filename if you want the README links to work in your new checkout. The PDF is a development artifact and is not copied into the Vite public directory.

## Configure the Vercel project

Import the completed source repository, choose Vite and set Root Directory to frontend. Enable inclusion of files outside that root during the build because frontend imports shared and prebuild uses backend manuscript tools. Use the configured install command npm ci && npm ci --prefix ../backend --omit=dev, build command npm run build and output directory dist.

The backend dependency install here supports read-only manuscript checks. It does not deploy Express. The backend/data source files and shared bundle must be available in the build environment. Only dist is served as the frontend application.

Set VITE_CONTENT_SOURCE=pdf and VITE_ANALYTICS_ENABLED=false for a reader-only release. Leave VITE_API_URL unset until there is a hosted HTTPS backend, then use that origin without /api and redeploy. Choose api content only when the backend is configured and intended to supply that content. Environment changes take effect at build time.

Disable Automatically expose System Environment Variables for this project because its strict validator rejects undeclared VITE_ names such as VITE_VERCEL_ENV. Configure Preview and Production intentionally; account previews need their own exact allowed origins on the backend.

## Preserve direct navigation

vercel.json rewrites application paths to index.html and excludes API paths, asset paths and paths ending in a file extension. After deployment, directly open and refresh /testament/freedom/10/14, /search?q=responsibility and /login. Confirm styles/images load and /assets/missing.js returns 404 rather than HTML.

If the backend is connected, complete registration, verification, login, account settings, private reading actions and logout through the deployed browser. Local server rendering, provider acceptance and a passing build are not evidence of that deployed flow. Record the resulting release URL, commit/source hash and chosen configuration names.

# 23 Troubleshoot by layer

## The build cannot find backend or shared files

Check construction stages and the frontend working directory. Preserve the sibling folder arrangement and Vercel outside-root setting. Install backend production dependencies for prebuild. Do not remove prebuild or duplicate shared data into arbitrary frontend files to hide the missing input.

## Production rejects a localhost API URL

Create the intended production-specific frontend environment or supply Vercel build values. A browser deployed on HTTPS cannot use the development machine's localhost. VITE_API_URL names the backend origin and must omit /api, credentials, query and fragment.

## API startup says configuration or storage is unavailable

Read the safe error code, then inspect NODE_ENV, runtime version, feature flags, database topology and required initialization steps. Production deliberately skips .env. With auth enabled, notification and account prerequisites must exist; community adds community/article checks. An empty database content store is not an approved published edition.

## An account request returns 401 403 or 404

401 usually requires a new valid session; remember that reload loses the in-memory access credential. 403 can mean unverified email or a missing capability. Owner-scoped 404 may intentionally conceal another reader's resource. Do not bypass these conditions by changing the browser's local user object.

## MongoDB transactions fail

Use a replica set or supported transactional cluster and the supplied initialization scripts. A successful ping to a standalone server does not prove transactional account/community readiness. Reproduce the behavior with isolated test fixtures before changing service transaction logic.

## A page is blank while lint and build pass

Inspect the imported component's default/named export and props, then run the route checks. A bundled module can still fail when React renders a route. Confirm current AppRoutes, ResourceState expectations and async service payload shapes. An empty scaffold file is not an implemented component.

## Email is accepted but not visible in the inbox

Check the provider account, authorized sender, recipient, Workspace policies and actual delivery evidence. Do not log raw verification/reset links while debugging. The Gmail adapter does not automatically retry uncertain sends; a timeout can occur after the provider accepted the message.

## Content verification fails

Compare the source manifest and exact archived inputs. Manual editing of shared/manuscript.json, draft artifacts or source text can invalidate the chain. Adopt revisions through the manuscript workflow. Missing text, accepted broken citations and intentional repeated passages are preserved source conditions, not bugs to normalize away.

# 24 Completion and independent handoff

## Review the finished reconstruction

Confirm that every staged source file matches the supplied manifest before making your own changes. Record package installation, backend tests, frontend tests, lint/build, manuscript/editorial/candidate checks and route-render checks separately. Check actual browser interaction and hosted behavior as separate observations.

The next appendices provide a current-code reference: locked dependency versions, every model's basic field/index inventory, all registered API routes, frontend route paths, complete worked modules and the staged file checklist. The attachments supply the full source for the modules not printed as listings and the manuscript/assets needed for exact reconstruction.

## Understand what can and cannot be reconstructed

The book and kit reconstruct the supplied application source and its local behavior. They do not recreate live OAuth grants, host accounts, production secrets, DNS control or a populated private database. Those require credentials and data owned by the operator. This distinction does not prevent a new local build; it defines which values the developer must create during the operating chapters.

## Continue after the baseline passes

Keep content authority, account ownership and publication states explicit when adding features. Add meaningful tests for new concurrency or permission behavior. Keep unavailable destinations honest, preserve the design tokens unless intentionally changing the design, and rerun route rendering when changing component imports or page assembly.

For a revised textbook edition, take a new source manifest and clearly record changed assumptions. The earlier historical documentation remains attached for context, but the ordered instructions in this edition target the current source snapshot. Dates, checksums and stated verification limits make that handoff reviewable by the next developer.

