## source-warning-157: printed punctuation

This is one consolidated warning, not one isolated typo. All observations remain as printed:

Punctuation kept exactly as printed (possible typos, not corrected)

- `?;` in Inquiry 31:6 ("a life?; every"), Freedom 28:7 ("collapse?; these"), Identity 7:1 ("they are?; many")
- `.".` (full stop after a closing quotation) in Inquiry 32:3, 34:4, 39:13, Freedom 34:2, Society 41:12, and `?"; â€¦ ?".` in Identity 13:29/14:29
- Quotation marks and apostrophes are mixed: straight `'` and `"` alongside curly `â€™ â€œ â€`. Each is kept as printed.
- Dashes: verses mostly use an unspaced em dash (`wordâ€”word`). Notes mostly use a spaced one (`word â€” word`). Both are kept.

| Example | PDF pages | Exact current verse |
| --- | --- | --- |
| inquiry:31:6 | 43 | Why then should the seeker not question the beliefs that govern a life?; every age has called its assumptions wisdom, and every age has later buried some of them as folly; what was unquestionable yesterday often becomes laughable tomorrow. |
| freedom:28:7 | 135 | Every meaningful discovery required someone willing to ask why. Why do the stars move? Why do seasons change? Why do people suffer? Why do societies flourish? Why do civilisations collapse?; these questions expanded understanding. They did not diminish it. |
| identity:7:1 | 340 | At the heart of identity lies a simple question: what makes a person who they are?; many answer with ancestry, nationality, tribe, or religion, yet these answers remain incomplete, for they describe origins more than outcomes, beginnings more than becoming. |
| inquiry:32:3 | 46 | Many have mistaken their explanations for reality; wisdom begins when a man says, "Perhaps I am mistaken.". |
| inquiry:34:4 | 51 | Arguments are born where humility has departed; blessed is the man who says, "This is what I believe," before saying, "This is what I know.". |
| inquiry:39:13 | 62 | Wisdom seeks to understand them all; be careful when saying, "This is what happened.". |
| freedom:34:2 | 160 | Pride says, "We have arrived."; wisdom says, "We still have far to travel.". |
| society:41:12 | 308 | Every generation is tempted to believe it has reached perfection; yet every generation leaves work unfinished; pride says, "We have arrived."; wisdom says, "We still have far to travel.". |
| identity:13:29 | 362, 363 | Ask yourself each day not, "What do others think of me?"; but, "What am I becoming?". |
| identity:14:29 | 367 | Ask yourself each day not, "What do others think of me?"; but, "What am I becoming?". |

Author decision: accepted. Mixed quote/apostrophe forms and dash styles are not normalized automatically.

